# CMake build output, wherever it is. An example builds under its own directory,
# so a rule anchored at the root would leave every example's output tracked — and
# once a build directory is in the index, a later rule does not take it back out.
build/
build-*/
cmake-build-*/
CMakeFiles/
CMakeCache.txt
cmake_install.cmake
install_manifest.txt
compile_commands.json
Makefile

# Generated configuration and staging output
/generated/
/stage/

# Python caches
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/

# Test and coverage output
Testing/
coverage/
*.gcda
*.gcno
*.gcov

# Local IDE and OS files
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

# The vendor's HAL and CMSIS sources, assembled by the toolchain for a build.
# They are fetched and are not part of this module: a checkout does not carry
# them, and a build brings the ones its board names. Nothing in here is
# hand-written.
#
# Anchored to the root on purpose. An unanchored `vendor/` matches at any depth,
# which swallowed `include/robohub/vendor/` — the platform's own header answering
# the vendor's — and left a file the build cannot compile without untracked and
# invisible. A rule that quietly swallows source is worse than one that ignores
# too much.
#
# This is also where a dependency's own `vendor/` folder is declared to live.
#
# `vendor/includes` is the exception, and the only thing here that is not a
# repository. The vendor's umbrella header includes a configuration header by a
# bare name — `#include "stm32f4xx_hal_conf.h"` — and the only version of that file
# anywhere is the vendor's `_template` and whatever somebody derived from it by
# hand. The toolchain derives it during a build, into this directory, so nothing
# in here is written by a person and nothing in here is tracked: a file kept in git
# is generated once and then stale for ever, which is the thing this replaced.
#
# The contents, not the directory itself: git does not look inside a directory it
# has excluded, so a rule that excluded `vendor/` could not be undone for anything
# beneath it.
#
# And nothing inside it is tracked either: every file here is generated during a
# build, so a file kept in git is generated once and then stale for ever, which is
# the thing generating them replaced.
/vendor/*
!/vendor/includes/
/vendor/includes/*

# Each example is a project in its own right and fetches its own vendor tree, so
# it has one of those too. Named rather than covered by an unanchored `vendor/`,
# which would swallow `include/robohub/vendor/` — this platform's own header
# answering the vendor's — and leave a file the build cannot compile without
# untracked and invisible.
examples/*/*/vendor/

# The configuration a build resolves. Written under the build directory, beside
# the sources that include it; this one is what the Python era left behind.
include/generated/

# Each example's resolved dependencies. Written by a build, like everything else a
# build produces.
#
# Ignored rather than tracked because an example inside this repository depends on
# this repository, so its lockfile pins the revision of the tree it is committed
# in: every commit would change it, and it would carry this machine's absolute
# paths to every checkout that took it. A resolution record that describes one
# checkout is not something to hand to another.
examples/*/*/rho.lock
